+---
+id: PLAN-013-wav-size-cap-rotation
+status: draft
+date: 2026-11-05
+review_count: 0
+patch: n/a
+---
+
+# PLAN-013: WAV recording size cap with rotation
+
+## Status
+
+draft
+
+## Context
+
+Session WAV recording (PLAN-010, PLAN-012) appends the same linear16 PCM
+that goes to the STT stream into a single `<stem>.wav` sibling. Long
+sessions grow that file without bound: at ~115 MB/hour of uncompressed
+16 kHz mono audio, a multi-hour session produces files that are awkward
+to store, copy, and open.
+
+The task asks for a configurable maximum file size (env or config,
+default 500 MB). When the cap is reached the current file is finalized
+and recording continues into a new file instead of stopping the session.
+Rotated names must make ordering obvious; every rotated file must be a
+valid playable WAV; and tests must cover rotation without writing
+hundreds of MB, so the cap must be injectable at a fine granularity.
+
+## Governing records
+
+- BDR-006: session audio is retained as a sibling WAV in the one-stem
+  bundle (`<stem>.md` / `<stem>.srt` / `<stem>.wav`). Rotation keeps the
+  first part at exactly `<stem>.wav`; additional parts are additive
+  siblings named `<stem>.part-NNN.wav`. See Risks for the bundle-shape
+  assumption.
+- ADR-007: the `.wav` path and the `audio_file` frontmatter key are
+  unchanged: `wavPathFor` still returns the part-1 path and `audio_file`
+  still holds its basename. The read path (`GET
+  /api/sessions/:id/audio`) continues to serve part 1.
+- ADR-005: tunables come from environment variables only, loaded through
+  `envSchema` in `server/config.ts` and documented in `.env.example`.
+  The cap follows that convention (`WAV_MAX_FILE_MB`); there is no
+  in-app settings surface.
+- ADR-006 / BDR-002: no new dependencies. Rotation is `node:fs/promises`
+  plus arithmetic inside the existing recorder.
+- ADR-001: the backend owns filesystem artifacts; the change lives in
+  `server/`. No web change is required.
+- PLAN-012: pause/resume semantics are preserved - dropped appends never
+  reach the write queue, so paused audio can neither land in a part nor
+  trigger a rotation.
+
+## Scope
+
+In scope:
+
+1. `server/config.ts`: new `WAV_MAX_FILE_MB` env var, default 500.
+2. `.env.example`: document the new variable.
+3. `server/audio/wav-recorder.ts`: `maxBytes` option on `WavRecorder`,
+   rotation into `<stem>.part-NNN.wav` parts, `partPaths` / `partCount`
+   getters, multi-part `abort()` cleanup.
+4. `server/session/manager.ts`: resolve the cap (config default or
+   `SessionManagerDeps.wavMaxBytes` test seam) and pass it to
+   `WavRecorder.create`.
+5. Automated tests: `server/audio/wav-recorder.test.ts`,
+   `server/config.test.ts`, `server/app.test.ts`.
+
+Out of scope:
+
+- Serving rotated parts over HTTP: `GET /api/sessions/:id/audio` keeps
+  returning part 1 (`<stem>.wav`). Multi-part playback/concatenation in
+  the Review page is a follow-up; the files are independently playable
+  on disk, which is what the task requires.
+- Frontmatter schema changes (`audio_files` list, part count). `audio_file`
+  keeps pointing at part 1 so `store.ts`, `routes.ts`, and the web types
+  are untouched.
+- Client-side (`web/`) changes of any kind.
+- Migrating or capping already-recorded sessions.
+
+## Contracts (literal)
+
+### `server/config.ts`
+
+```ts
+WAV_MAX_FILE_MB: z.coerce.number().int().positive().default(500),
+```
+
+- Placed in `envSchema` next to `STT_IDLE_TIMEOUT_S` (same
+  unit-suffixed naming convention).
+- The value is a whole number of mebibytes; byte-level caps for tests
+  come through the `wavMaxBytes` dep, not the env var.
+
+### `server/audio/wav-recorder.ts`
+
+```ts
+export interface WavRecorderOptions {
+  sampleRate?: number;
+  channels?: number;
+  bitsPerSample?: number;
+  maxBytes?: number; // max on-disk bytes per part, including the 44-byte header
+}
+
+// WavRecorder
+get partPaths(): readonly string[]; // every part created so far, in order
+get partCount(): number;            // === partPaths.length
+```
+
+Pinned semantics:
+
+- Part naming, pinned exactly (part 1 keeps the ADR-007 bundle name):
+
+  ```ts
+  function partPathFor(filePath: string, part: number): string {
+    if (part === 1) return filePath;
+    const stem = filePath.replace(/\.wav$/i, '');
+    return `${stem}.part-${String(part).padStart(3, '0')}.wav`;
+  }
+  ```
+
+  So `session.wav` rotates into `session.part-002.wav`,
+  `session.part-003.wav`, ... Ordering is obvious to a reader: the bare
+  stem is part 1, the numeric suffix counts up from there. Each part is
+  opened with the same `wx` flag, so a name collision fails loudly
+  rather than clobbering.
+- `maxBytes` bounds total on-disk size per part, header included, so the
+  PCM payload capacity per part is `maxBytes - HEADER_SIZE`. Every
+  produced file is `<= maxBytes` bytes. `maxBytes: undefined` disables
+  rotation entirely (single file, current behavior).
+- Validation: `create` throws a `RangeError` when `maxBytes` is defined
+  and `< HEADER_SIZE + blockAlign` (46 bytes for the default format) -
+  a cap that cannot hold one sample frame is a config bug, and failing
+  the session start is more honest than recording nothing.
+- Writes split at sample boundaries, never mid-sample. The enqueued
+  append op loops over the chunk: `room = payloadCap - payloadBytes`;
+  if `room < blockAlign`, rotate first (finalize current part, open the
+  next, reset `payloadBytes`); then write
+  `min(view.length, floor(room / blockAlign) * blockAlign)` bytes and
+  repeat. Every part payload is therefore a multiple of `blockAlign`
+  and `<= payloadCap`.
+- Rotation runs inside the same serialized op as the write, on the
+  existing queue: a rotate can never interleave with another append or
+  with `finalize`/`abort`.
+- Rotating finalizes the previous part fully (RIFF/data sizes patched,
+  handle closed) before the next part is opened, so at most one part is
+  ever open and every completed part is a valid standalone WAV.
+- `bytesWritten` keeps its meaning: total PCM payload bytes written,
+  summed across all parts. Dropped (paused) appends still count
+  nothing.
+- `pause()` / `resume()` / `isPaused` are unchanged: the call-time
+  `paused` gate drops chunks before enqueueing, so paused audio can
+  never trigger a rotation.
+- `finalize()` patches and closes only the current part (earlier parts
+  were already finalized at rotation) and stays idempotent.
+- `abort()` closes the current handle and unlinks every path in
+  `partPaths`, not just the base file - no orphaned parts after a
+  failed session start.
+- A rotation-time fs failure (disk full, `wx` collision) rejects the
+  `append` promise. `SessionManager.sendAudio` already treats any
+  append rejection as `recorderFailed` and continues the session
+  unrecorded; parts finalized before the failure remain valid files.
+
+### `server/session/manager.ts`
+
+- `SessionManagerDeps` gains `wavMaxBytes?: number` - the same kind of
+  test seam as `providerFactory` et al. `buildServer(config, deps)`
+  forwards it untouched, so `app.test.ts` can inject a tiny cap.
+- The manager stores the deps object and resolves the cap lazily inside
+  `start()` (not in the constructor) so a test may mutate
+  `deps.wavMaxBytes` after `buildServer` but before `POST
+  /api/sessions`:
+
+  ```ts
+  const maxBytes =
+    deps.wavMaxBytes ?? this.config.WAV_MAX_FILE_MB * 1024 * 1024;
+  recorder = await WavRecorder.create(wavPath, { maxBytes });
+  ```
+
+- `meta.audio_file` keeps `path.basename(wavPath)` (part 1). `finish()`
+  keeps calling `recorder.finalize()` once; the recorder finalizes
+  whichever part is open.
+- `sendAudio`, `pause`, `resume` need no changes.
+
+### `.env.example`
+
+Add after the `STT_IDLE_TIMEOUT_S` block:
+
+```
+# WAV recording rotation: maximum size per .wav file (MiB) before the
+# recorder finalizes it and continues into <stem>.part-NNN.wav siblings.
+WAV_MAX_FILE_MB=500
+```
+
+## Steps
+
+### Step 1 - `server/config.ts` + `.env.example`
+
+- Add `WAV_MAX_FILE_MB` to `envSchema` per the contract above.
+- Add the documented block to `.env.example`.
+
+### Step 2 - `server/audio/wav-recorder.ts`
+
+- Extend `WavRecorderOptions` with `maxBytes`; validate it in `create`.
+- Track `partPaths: string[]`, `payloadBytes` per part, `totalBytes`
+  across parts; `bytesWritten` returns `totalBytes`.
+- Implement the split/rotate write loop inside the existing enqueued
+  op; extract the shared "patch sizes + close" logic so both rotation
+  and `finalize()` use it.
+- `partPaths` / `partCount` getters; `abort()` unlinks all parts.
+
+### Step 3 - `server/session/manager.ts`
+
+- Add `wavMaxBytes?: number` to `SessionManagerDeps`; retain the deps
+  object on the instance.
+- In `start()`, resolve and pass `maxBytes` to `WavRecorder.create` per
+  the contract above.
+
+### Step 4 - tests
+
+- Extend `server/audio/wav-recorder.test.ts`, `server/config.test.ts`,
+  and `server/app.test.ts` per the test plan.
+
+## Test plan
+
+Automated (vitest), new cases; caps are injected in bytes so nothing
+writes more than a few hundred bytes.
+
+`server/audio/wav-recorder.test.ts`:
+
+- "rotates into part files that each stay under the cap": `maxBytes`
+  set so payload capacity is 100 bytes (`maxBytes = 144`); append 250
+  bytes in several chunks; assert `clip.wav`, `clip.part-002.wav`,
+  `clip.part-003.wav` exist, each parses as a WAV (RIFF/WAVE/fmt/data
+  markers), each header's patched sizes match its real payload
+  (`readUInt32LE(40)`), each file is `<= maxBytes` long, and the
+  concatenated payloads equal the appended stream in order.
+- "splits a single oversize chunk at the cap boundary": one 250-byte
+  append with payload cap 100 produces parts of exactly 100, 100, and
+  50 payload bytes; `bytesWritten === 250`.
+- "keeps parts sample-aligned when the cap is not a multiple of
+  blockAlign": payload cap 101 with default format (blockAlign 2)
+  produces part payloads of 100, never 101.
+- "partPaths / partCount reflect rotation order": after two rotations,
+  `partCount === 3` and `partPaths` lists base then `.part-002` then
+  `.part-003`; with no `maxBytes`, `partCount === 1` and
+  `partPaths === [filePath]`.
+- "paused appends neither count toward the cap nor trigger rotation":
+  pause, append a chunk larger than the cap, resume, append within the
+  cap - still one part.
+- "abort removes every rotated part": append past the cap, abort, all
+  paths in `partPaths` are gone.
+- "rejects a cap too small to hold one frame":
+  `create(path, { maxBytes: 45 })` rejects (RangeError).
+
+`server/config.test.ts`:
+
+- `WAV_MAX_FILE_MB` defaults to 500 when unset; coerces a string value
+  (`'250'` -> 250); rejects `0`, negative, and non-numeric values.
+
+`server/app.test.ts` (in the `session audio recording` describe):
+
+- "rotates the session WAV when the cap is reached": `beforeEach` is
+  adjusted to hold the `SessionManagerDeps` object in a variable the
+  test can mutate; the test sets `deps.wavMaxBytes` (e.g. 300 -> 256
+  bytes of payload room) after `buildServer`, starts a session with
+  `recordAudio: true`, sends ~600 bytes of PCM in a few chunks, waits
+  for `provider.audio`, then asserts: `wavPathFor(dir, id)` exists and
+  is a valid size-patched WAV `<= wavMaxBytes`, the sibling
+  `.part-002.wav` exists and is a valid size-patched WAV, concatenated
+  payloads equal the sent bytes, `POST /stop` succeeds (the session is
+  not torn down by rotation), and `meta.audio_file` still names the
+  base `.wav` while `GET /api/sessions/:id/audio` still serves part 1.
+
+Existing tests that must keep passing: all of
+`wav-recorder.test.ts` (no-`maxBytes` path is the old single-file
+behavior), the `session audio recording` describe, and the full suite.
+
+Gates: `npm test`, `npm run typecheck`.
+
+## Risks and notes
+
+- Assumption (recorded per task instruction): the ADR-007 bundle is a
+  three-file `<stem>` contract and this plan adds a fourth+ file shape
+  (`<stem>.part-NNN.wav`) when a session actually rotates. This is
+  treated as an additive extension within the task's scope; naming part
+  1 `<stem>.part-001.wav` instead was considered and rejected because it
+  renames the pinned `<stem>.wav` artifact and would ripple into
+  `wavPathFor`, `audio_file`, the audio route, and existing tests. If
+  the owner wants multi-part bundles formalized, that is a candidate
+  for a new ADR - flagged rather than silently violated, per the
+  precedence rule.
+- Assumption: "maximum file size" is enforced as a hard cap including
+  the 44-byte header, so every file is `<= maxBytes`; chunks are split
+  at `blockAlign` boundaries rather than letting a file overshoot the
+  cap by up to one chunk. A part can end a few bytes under the cap when
+  the cap is not sample-aligned.
+- Assumption: the unit is MiB (`* 1024 * 1024`) consistent with the
+  "~115 MB/hour" figure in BDR-005/BDR-006 context.
+- Lexicographic caveat: `session.part-002.wav` sorts before
+  `session.wav` in a plain directory listing (`p` < `w`). Ordering is
+  still obvious from the names (bare stem = part 1, suffix counts up),
+  which is what the task asks for.
+- Review playback serves part 1 only (out of scope). A rotated
+  session's Review page plays the first ~cap of audio; a follow-up can
+  expose `partPaths` through a route or frontmatter list.
+- `bytesWritten` reverts to a cross-part total; the existing tests that
+  read it before `finalize` use single-part recordings and are
+  unaffected.
+- Rotation does not fix the pre-existing "unfinalized WAV reports
+  `dataSize: 0` after a crash" limitation (PLAN-010 Risks); a crashed
+  session can now leave several valid parts plus one unfinalized tail.
+- Pre-existing test flake (carried over from PLAN-012): `REST + WS
+  surface > full session lifecycle` asserts exact millisecond cue times
+  that can drift by 1 ms; unrelated to this plan - re-run before
+  treating the gate as failed.
+
+## Definition of Done
+
+- [ ] `WAV_MAX_FILE_MB` is parsed by `envSchema` with default 500 and is
+  documented in `.env.example`.
+- [ ] `WavRecorder` accepts `maxBytes`, rotates into
+  `<stem>.part-NNN.wav` parts (part 1 unchanged at `<stem>.wav`), and
+  every produced file is a valid standalone WAV `<= maxBytes`.
+- [ ] Reaching the cap finalizes the current part and continues
+  recording; the session is never stopped by rotation.
+- [ ] `SessionManager` resolves the cap from `WAV_MAX_FILE_MB`,
+  overridable via `SessionManagerDeps.wavMaxBytes` for tests.
+- [ ] `partPaths` / `partCount` expose rotated parts; `abort()` removes
+  all of them; `meta.audio_file` and the `/audio` route keep pointing
+  at part 1.
+- [ ] New unit, config, and end-to-end tests pass with byte-scale caps;
+  `npm test` and `npm run typecheck` are clean.
